    "slot_value": "Approval would need to come from a designated senior authority, such as the incident commander, emergency management lead, or an executive designee, depending on the situation and agency policy. If there’s time, legal or privacy review should be included before it goes out. It should only happen when waiting for legal or privacy review would clearly worsen the emergency, such as a fast-moving incident where seconds or minutes matter and the designated reviewer isn’t available in time. In that case, the authority can act first to protect life or critical operations, but only within a tightly defined emergency role. Post-event approval should be used only when the exception was necessary to keep the service functioning during an urgent situation, or when the need wasn’t reasonably knowable before the event. It also makes sense when waiting for approval would have disrupted a critical agency operation or caused a service outage for others.",
